  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two mountain-lake settings under changing climate conditions and links them to ecological responses. In the background, glaciers, rainfall, and a labeled glacial lake outburst suggest stronger hydrologic inputs from glacier melt and increased precipitation. On the left is a closed lake fed by an input river but lacking an outlet. Arrows and labels indicate infiltration into the ground, rising greenhouse-gas release, and changes in water properties: salinity appears to decrease while water level and transparency increase. On the right is an exorheic lake connected to both an input river and an output river, with strong evaporation shown above it.\n\nTogether, the scene emphasizes how basin type controls lake behavior. The closed basin seems more sensitive to internal accumulation and subsurface exchange, whereas the exorheic lake is more directly flushed by river inflow and outflow. The dashed boxes connect the lakes to a small underwater inset showing aquatic plants and labels for phytoplankton and microbes, implying that hydrological shifts affect lake ecosystems and microbial production. Overall, the image conveys a climate-driven cascade from mountain precipitation, glacier melt, and evaporation to lake level, chemistry, greenhouse gases, and biological communities.",
